      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Transportation by Rivers"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Transportation by rivers involves moving weathered and eroded rock material downstream towards lower elevations.",
        "Deposition takes place when a river's velocity and energy decrease, causing it to drop its carried load.",
        "The interplay between river transportation capacity and deposition leads to various fluvial landforms."
      ],
      "id": "gex2kjr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6v29gvv",
      "content": "• Traction – rolling of large boulders."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4i8vn8i",
      "content": "• Saltation – bouncing of small pebbles."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xn6qfcr",
      "content": "• Suspension – fine particles carried in water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k57sipp",
      "content": "• Solution – dissolved load."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Exams often test the four modes of river load transport: solution, suspension, saltation, and traction.",
        "Differentiate between depositional landforms like floodplains, meanders, alluvial fans, and deltas."
      ],
      "id": "2onfqhy"
    },
    {
      "id": "gvo39la",
